y remain in isolation  after testing positive for kovit 19. the  american election  is now just about a month away we'll  have all that and more  on the west block robin all right thanks  for that mercedes  thousands of people gathered in montreal  today to demand justice for joyce  eshequan  supporters held a peaceful march in the  city the quebec government has promised  an inquiry  into the indigenous woman's care at a  hospital eshequan recorded herself while  she was visibly distressed  and calling for help staff at the  hospital were caught on tape  taunting her with insulting remarks  shortly before she died  a nurse and an orderly have since been  fired  police in nova scotia are investigating  a hate crime  after a sign directing visitors to the  headstone of viola desmond  a canadian civil rights pioneer was  vandalized  the sign in camp hill cemetery was  marked with three  highly offensive racial slurs but they  have since been removed  in 1946 desmond was arrested for  refusing to leave a whites-only section  of a movie theater  it's one of the most high-profile cases  of racial discrimination  in canadian history coming up nearly 24  people are missing in france and italy  as storm alex pounds europe  venice felt the brunt of what's known as  storm alex  the low-lying city is particularly  vulnerable to this kind of weather  newly installed flood barriers were put  to the test and have  appeared to held back high tides earlier  france was hit with the system's fierce  winds and torrential rain  nearly 500 millimeters of rain fell in  just 12 hours near nice  france usually gets about 600  millimeters of rain  all year the mayor of knees says this is  the worst flooding  in a century millions of people living  in  and around the spanish capital are under  another strict lockdown  as cases surge madrid has become  europe's biggest hot spot  and to deal with the spiking cases  spain's government has imposed  two weeks of new restrictions among the  new rules  restaurants must stop serving food and  drinks after 10 pm  and no one is allowed to travel in and  out of the city  unless it's absolutely necessary  tighter restrictions have also been  imposed in the u.k  where cases are rising fast in northern  england it's now illegal to meet people  indoors from other households the new  rules went into action  as hundreds of university students in  the city of newcastle  tested positive for coven 19.  still ahead an ontario father's  determination to get help for his son  who has a rare disease  months ahead of their son's third  birthday an ontario couple is determined  to get him a life saving gift  the young boy has a rare disease with no  cure  it could leave him paralyzed by the age  of 10. treatment costs millions and the  pandemic halt of the family's  fundraising plan  but as karen lieberman explains nothing  can stop their perseverance  these can be isolating times for anyone  let alone the family of a child with an  ultra rare disease  michael piravalakis is the only child in  canada  with [ __ ] paraplegia type 50 spg  50 for short tolbert has been the  hardest point in my entire life right we  got  i'm working my wife is not working  anymore we have you know  we're worried about our bills worried  about how we're going to pay for  the future let's go keep going we first  met him at 18 months shortly after he  was diagnosed with a neurodegenerative  disease  that will rob him of all functions one  by one  i stopped praying for a while and i was  like you know why would they do this to  him why would he have this disease there  was no hope  no hope in a race against time the  toronto family decided to crowdfund a  cure  researchers believe gene therapy has the  potential to halt the progression of spg  50 and even reverse some of the damage  but there are no guarantees for the 60  or so people believed to have the  disease worldwide  even so michael's family and others  believe this is their only  shot as nobody has ever been treated  this way for the disease  before the covid19 pandemic has impacted  everyone in some way  shape or form and michael is no  exception his therapies were halted  and the fundraising to help save him put  on hold  so far more than one and a half million  dollars of a three million dollar goal  has been raised  that's just to get the initial clinical  trial ready for michael and up to 10  others in about a year  the family anticipates they will need  more financial help down the line  but they're only fundraising now for  their immediate needs  i think if koba didn't happen we would  have raised 2 million dollars this year  for the funding  for months from home they watched  michael's disease progress  you see that he's more in his tippy toes  rather than on his feet because the  spasticity is kicking in the paralysis  is kicking in and that's related to  being in quarantine for six months with  no therapy  so terry decided despite the pandemic  his son  must be saved so i started training and  i thought maybe i could get from here to  ottawa and we decided that we're going  to see if we can meet mr trudeau  a 400 kilometer bike ride from pickering  ontario to the nation's capital  with the goal of meeting the prime  minister and urging him to contribute to  the fundraising efforts  it's a scary thought to say that you're  so close  and just because of money you can't get  your kid treated  the journey is a costly one both  emotionally  and financially but at the heart of it  michael  a child whose future hangs in the  balance karen lieberman  global news toronto  the green party pioneer up next  elizabeth may's  impact on canada's political landscape  the green party of canada will have a  new leader thousands of members are  voting today for one of eight candidates  vying for the top job  the winner will succeed the woman who  has been the face of the party  for more than a decade eric sorensen has  a look back at the long career  of elizabeth may i organized an  environment club in my high school  long before she discovered politics  elizabeth may found the cause that would  shape her life  protecting the environment in the 70s  she organized against pesticide spraying  in her home of cape breton  in the 80s the environmental lawyer  worked on canada's earliest  climate policies on acid rain and ozone  depletion  in the government of brian mulroney  there was no doubt in my mind nor theirs  that we had to act on the climate crisis  she became a national public figure  founded the sierra club  and championed environmental awareness  to protest the infamous tar pons in cape  breton she once undertook a hunger  strike on parliament hill it was a  personal decision  that as an act of desperation soon she  would take the fight for the environment  inside the halls of parliament first by  winning the leadership of the small  often ignored green party  [Applause]  elizabeth was never in politics for  politics she always wanted to make the  world a better place particularly by  solving our serious environmental  problems  and i think she realized when the green  party leadership came open  that that was a vehicle to help her do  the right thing failing to win a seat in  her first attempt  may finally found fertile soil for a  green politician  on vancouver island and that was an  historic turning point in canadian  politics  i stand here today as the first elected  green member of parliament in canadian  history  may brought her fight to parliament and  fought her way into the national debates  you just sweep it across the table don't  you an important victory for women  and for the issue she put at the center  of canada's political discourse  she has faced controversies the views of  some green members  at a times her own comments to be  humiliated like the rest of us schmucks  but may spearheaded legislation on lyme  disease  and a ban on whales in captivity and has  worked across party lines to find  common 